{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06415","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06415","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06415","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelika","familyName":"Romanou","name":"Romanou, Angelika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gail","familyName":"Weiss","name":"Weiss, Gail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"Bosselut","name":"Bosselut, Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PERK: Long-Context Reasoning as Test-Time Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T21:38:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:07:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-30T20:34:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-01T01:44:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T15:26:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T02:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-context reasoning requires accurately identifying relevant information in extensive, noisy input contexts. In this work, we propose PERK (Parameter Efficient Reasoning over Knowledge), a scalable approach for learning to encode long contexts using gradient updates at test time. Specifically, PERK employs two nested optimization loops in a meta-training phase. The inner loop rapidly encodes contexts into a low-rank adapter (LoRA) that serves as a parameter-efficient memory module for the base model. Concurrently, the outer loop learns to use the updated adapter to accurately recall and reason over relevant information from the encoded long context. Our evaluations on several long-context reasoning tasks show that PERK significantly outperforms the standard long-context finetuning, achieving average absolute performance gains of up to 20% for Qwen-2.5 (0.5B \u0026amp; 7B) on synthetic and real-world long-context reasoning. PERK also maintains its advantages across model scales and families. Compared to specialized long-context LLMs, PERK matches or surpasses their performance. Finally, our analyses show PERK is more robust to reasoning complexity, length extrapolation, and the positions of relevant information in contexts. https://perk-long-context.web.app"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026, 10 pages, 7 figures, test-time learning, long context, meta-learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06415","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:29:45Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:29:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T04:59:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06457","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06457","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06457","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dustin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dustin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui-Jie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Rui-Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Abreu","name":"Abreu, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taylor","familyName":"Kergan","name":"Kergan, Taylor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhong","familyName":"Chou","name":"Chou, Yuhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jibin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Eshraghian","name":"Eshraghian, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Systematic Analysis of Hybrid Linear Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T23:54:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:10:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:07:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T00:15:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers face quadratic complexity and memory issues with long sequences, prompting the adoption of linear attention mechanisms using fixed-size hidden states. However, linear models often suffer from limited recall performance, leading to hybrid architectures that combine linear and full attention layers. Despite extensive hybrid architecture research, the choice of linear attention component has not been deeply explored. We systematically evaluate various linear attention models across generations - vector recurrences to advanced gating mechanisms - both standalone and hybridized. To enable this comprehensive analysis, we trained and open-sourced 72 models: 36 at 340M parameters (20B tokens) and 36 at 1.3B parameters (100B tokens), covering six linear attention variants across five hybridization ratios. Benchmarking on standard language modeling and recall tasks reveals that superior standalone linear models do not necessarily excel in hybrids. While language modeling remains stable across linear-to-full attention ratios, recall significantly improves with increased full attention layers, particularly below a 3:1 ratio. Our study highlights selective gating, hierarchical recurrence, and controlled forgetting as critical for effective hybrid models. We recommend architectures such as HGRN-2 or GatedDeltaNet with a linear-to-full ratio between 3:1 and 6:1 to achieve Transformer-level recall efficiently. Our models are open-sourced at https://huggingface.co/collections/m-a-p/hybrid-linear-attention-research-686c488a63d609d2f20e2b1e."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06457","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:30:48Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:30:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:17:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06567","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06567","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06567","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaibin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kaibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SlimCaching: Edge Caching of Mixture-of-Experts for Distributed Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T05:43:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:21:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T09:35:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:28:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T09:41:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:06:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models improve the scalability of large language models (LLMs) by activating only a small subset of relevant experts per input. However, the sheer number of expert networks in an MoE model introduces a significant storage/memory burden for an edge device. To address this challenge, we consider a scenario where experts are dispersed across an edge network for distributed inference. Based on the popular Top-$K$ expert selection strategy, we formulate a latency minimization problem by optimizing expert caching on edge servers under storage constraints. When $K=1$, the problem reduces to a monotone submodular maximization problem with knapsack constraints, for which we design a greedy-based algorithm with a $(1 - 1/e)$-approximation guarantee. For the general case where $K\\geq1$, expert co-activation within the same MoE layer introduces non-submodularity, which renders greedy methods ineffective. To tackle this issue, we propose a successive greedy decomposition method to decompose the original problem into a series of subproblems, with each being solved by a dynamic programming approach. Furthermore, we design an accelerated algorithm based on the max-convolution technique to obtain the approximate solution with a provable guarantee in polynomial time. Simulation results on various MoE models demonstrate that our method significantly reduces inference latency compared to existing baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 11 figures. This paper has been accepted by IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06567","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:33:35Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:33:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:39:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06313","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06313","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06313","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiarash","familyName":"Zahirnia","name":"Zahirnia, Kiarash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zahra","familyName":"Golpayegani","name":"Golpayegani, Zahra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walid","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Walid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ETT: Expanding the Long Context Understanding Capability of LLMs at Test-Time"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-08T18:06:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:01:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-15T13:47:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-16T00:48:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T23:17:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:12:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based Language Models' computation and memory overhead increase quadratically as a function of sequence length. The quadratic cost poses challenges when employing LLMs for processing long sequences. In this work, we introduce \\ourmodelacronym~(Extend at Test-Time), method for extending the context length of short context Transformer-based LLMs, with constant memory requirement and linear computation overhead. ETT enable the extension of the context length at test-time by efficient fine-tuning the model's parameters on the input context, chunked into overlapping small subsequences. We evaluate ETT on LongBench by extending the context length of GPT-Large and Phi-2 up to 32 times, increasing from 1k to 32k tokens. This results in up to a 30 percent improvement in the model's accuracy. We also study how context can be stored in LLM's weights effectively and efficiently. Through a detailed ablation study, we examine which Transformer modules are most beneficial to fine-tune at test-time. Interestingly, we find that fine-tuning the second layer of the FFNs is more effective than full fine-tuning, leading to a further improvement in the models' accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06313","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:27:14Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:27:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-23T02:14:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06608","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06608","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06608","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxiang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Xiaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Colin","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Colin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjia","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Junjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nexus:Proactive Intra-GPU Disaggregation of Prefill and Decode in LLM Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T07:27:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:25:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T15:48:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-11T00:42:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-15T01:17:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-16T00:15:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-16T01:05:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-07-17T00:12:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-08-07T12:26:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-08-08T00:37:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Monolithic serving with chunked prefill improves GPU utilization by batching prefill and decode together, but suffers from fine-grained phase interference. Engine-level prefill-decode (PD) disaggregation avoids interference but incurs higher hardware and coordination overhead. Prior intra-GPU disaggregation approaches multiplex prefill and decode within a single GPU, using SLO-based tuning guided by heuristics from offline profiling or reactive feedback loops. However, these methods respond reactively to performance issues rather than anticipating them, limiting adaptability under dynamic workloads.\n We ask: can we achieve proactive intra-GPU disaggregation that adapts effectively to dynamic workloads? The key challenge lies in managing the conflicting resource demands of prefill and decode under varying conditions. We first show that GPU resources exhibit diminishing returns -- beyond a saturation point, more allocation yields minimal latency benefit. Second, we observe that memory bandwidth contention becomes a critical bottleneck. These insights motivate a design that dynamically partitions GPU resources across prefill and decode phases, while jointly considering compute capacity, memory footprint, and bandwidth contention.\n Evaluated on diverse LLMs and workloads, our system Nexus achieves up to 2.2x higher throughput, 20x lower TTFT, and 2.5x lower TBT than vLLM; outperforms SGLang by up to 2x; and matches or exceeds disaggregated vLLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06608","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:34:37Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:34:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-08T02:01:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06517","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.06517","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.06517","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zicong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Luohe","name":"Luohe, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuchao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zuchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoyuan","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Baoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lefei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lefei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpindleKV: A Novel KV Cache Reduction Method Balancing Both Shallow and Deep Layers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-09T03:33:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:16:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved impressive accomplishments in recent years. However, the increasing memory consumption of KV cache has possessed a significant challenge to the inference system. Eviction methods have revealed the inherent redundancy within the KV cache, demonstrating its potential for reduction, particularly in deeper layers. However, KV cache reduction for shallower layers has been found to be insufficient. Based on our observation that, the KV cache exhibits a high degree of similarity. Based on this observation, we proposed a novel KV cache reduction method, SpindleKV, which balances both shallow and deep layers. For deep layers, we employ an attention weight based eviction method, while for shallow layers, we apply a codebook based replacement approach which is learnt by similarity and merging policy. Moreover, SpindleKV addressed the Grouped-Query Attention (GQA) dilemma faced by other attention based eviction methods. Experiments on two common benchmarks with three different LLMs shown that SpindleKV obtained better KV cache reduction effect compared to baseline methods, while preserving similar or even better model performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACL 2025 main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06517","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-10T01:32:20Z","registered":"2025-07-10T01:32:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-10T01:32:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":6,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=doi%3a%2810.48550%2farXiv.2507.06313+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06415+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06457+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06517+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06567+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2507.06608%29\u0026page[size]=6"}}